From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:33:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Put the Placidus limit at 90 degrees minus the true
 obliquity

placidusCusps refused every latitude beyond 66 degrees. The semi-arcs are
undefined only inside the polar circle, where |latitude| >= 90 - epsilon.
That limit runs from 66.533 to 66.589 degrees over 1800-2200.

The ladder is preregistered in zodiacs.org's repository (brief v1 rule 1h):
336 cases at +/-66.05 to +/-66.55 degrees, every three hours on 21 June
1800, 2000 and 2200. There the engine refused all 336, where Swiss Ephemeris
computes 320. Its whole-sign fallback differed from Swiss's Placidus by up
to 147.5 degrees (audit ledger angles-houses-aspects-3).

The limit is now 90 - epsilon, with epsilon the true obliquity the angles
already use. The ladder test checks that each case falls on the same side of
ERFA's limit as the arbiter: 320 are computed and 16 refused. Each computed
chart has twelve distinct cusps in order. With the old limit the test fails.

Measured here against Swiss 2.10.03 on its own inputs (ARMC and true
obliquity), and not committed: the status agrees on 336 of 336 cases, and
the cusps are within 0.0085".

Not in this change: naming the fallback system and offering Porphyry, the
rest of rule 1h. Both change the public house-system list and the receipt.
They wait for a decision on whether the polar fallback stays whole sign or
becomes Porphyry, as it is in Swiss.

496 tests pass.

